## Generated C++ code analysis

Setting `TORCH_COMPILE_DEBUG=1`, the inductor-generated C++ kernel reveals the root cause:

```cpp
// bounds check references tmp2 BEFORE it is declared:
TORCH_CHECK((at::vec::VecMask<int64_t,2>::set(
    at::vec::VecMask<int64_t,2>::from(1),
    (at::vec::VecMask<int64_t,2>(tmp2 < at::vec::VectorizedN<int64_t,2>(ks1))),  // ← tmp2 used here
    static_cast<int64_t>(9L)
)).all_masked(), "index out of bounds: tmp2 < ks1");

// ... tmp2 is declared AFTER the check:
auto tmp0 = x0;
auto tmp1 = c10::convert<int64_t>(tmp0);
auto tmp2 = at::vec::VectorizedN<int64_t,2>::arange(tmp1, 1);  // ← declared here, too late
```

The C++ codegen emits bounds-check statements at the **top** of the vectorized loop body, before any `tmpN` variable declarations. But the check expression depends on `tmp2`, which is assigned later. This is a statement ordering bug — the bounds check should be emitted **after** the variables it references are declared.


